PHASE 3: RESTORE MULTIPLE SHIPMENTS TO DIFFERENT DESTINATIONS

This is a major Swiftvero feature and must be implemented properly.

Customers should be able to create several separate shipments in one booking session without repeatedly entering the same sender and collection address.

Create a feature called:

“Send Multiple Shipments”

Example scenario

A customer in Lagos wants to send:

* One package to London.
* One package to Toronto.
* One package to New York.

The customer should enter their own sender details and collection address once.

They should then add the individual shipments and enter each recipient’s details separately.

Recommended booking structure

Create a parent booking group with separate child shipment records.

Parent booking group

The group should contain:

* Unique booking group ID.
* Customer ID.
* Sender details.
* Shared collection address, where applicable.
* Pickup method.
* Scheduled pickup information.
* Group status.
* Total payable amount.
* Creation date.

Individual shipment records

Each child shipment must contain:

* Unique internal shipment ID.
* Unique Swiftvero shipment code.
* Recipient name.
* Recipient phone number.
* Destination country.
* Destination city.
* Full delivery address.
* Package description.
* Declared contents.
* Package quantity or package count.
* Estimated weight.
* Verified actual weight.
* Dimensions.
* Individual quotation.
* Payment allocation.
* Carrier reference or references.
* Individual tracking history.
* Individual shipment status.

Each destination must be a separate shipment.

Do not place several different destinations inside one ordinary shipment record.

Customer interface

The booking process should work as follows:

Step 1: Sender details.

Ask for sender information and the collection address once.

Allow customers to select an existing saved address.

Step 2: Add shipments.

Show an individual shipment section.

Each shipment should collect the correct recipient and package information.

Provide a clear:

“Add another shipment”

action.

Allow customers to edit or remove an individual shipment before confirmation.

Step 3: Collection arrangement.

If all packages share the same collection address, allow one collection arrangement for the entire group.

If destinations are different, preserve separate shipment records.

Where packages have different collection addresses, group them into separate collection jobs and explain any resulting scheduling or pricing differences.

Step 4: Review.

Show a clear group summary.

Display:

* Number of shipments.
* Sender information.
* Each destination.
* Each recipient.
* Package descriptions.
* Selected collection method.
* Available quotations.
* Total payable amount when all quotations are ready.

Pricing and payment

Each shipment must have its own quotation.

Show individual costs clearly.

Where the existing payment architecture supports it, allow the customer to pay for several approved quotations in one checkout.

The combined checkout must retain a separate payment allocation for each shipment.

Do not merge several shipments into one vague total with no breakdown.

Do not mark all shipments paid when payment has only been successfully allocated to some of them.

If combined payment is unsupported, allow the customer to pay approved shipment quotations individually without losing the parent booking group.

Tracking

Each shipment should have its own Swiftvero shipment code and tracking history.

The parent booking group should allow the customer to view all its child shipments in one place.

Do not use one tracking timeline for packages travelling to different destinations.

